-- Q1: easy / L1 — ESPP $25,000 annual purchase limitation
(6, 'Individual Tax Planning: Compensation', $EXPL$Under Section 423, what is the maximum fair market value of stock an employee may purchase through a qualified employee stock purchase plan in any calendar year?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$25,000, based on fair market value at the grant date", "$50,000, based on fair market value at the purchase date", "$100,000, matching the ISO first-exercisable limit", "There is no statutory dollar limit on ESPP purchases"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Section 423(b)(8) limits the value of stock purchasable under a qualified ESPP to $25,000 per calendar year, measured by the stock's FMV at the time the option is granted. This prevents the favorable tax treatment from applying to unlimited amounts. Wrong (B): The $50,000 figure is not an ESPP threshold, and the measurement date is the grant date, not the purchase date. Wrong (C): The $100,000 limit applies to ISOs under Section 422(d), not to ESPPs under Section 423. Wrong (D): Section 423(b)(8) explicitly imposes a $25,000 annual limit on ESPP purchases.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q2: medium / L2 — ISO $100K first-exercisable limit excess becomes NQSO
(6, 'Individual Tax Planning: Compensation', $EXPL$Birchwood Technologies grants employee Hana Morales ISOs for 8,000 shares at $15 per share on January 1, 2026, when FMV is also $15. All 8,000 options first become exercisable on January 1, 2027. How are the options classified for tax purposes in 2027?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["All 8,000 options are treated as ISOs because the grant meets all Section 422 requirements", "The first 6,666 options ($99,990) are treated as ISOs and the remaining 1,334 are treated as NQSOs", "All 8,000 options are recharacterized as NQSOs because the total exceeds $100,000", "The first 1,334 options are treated as NQSOs and the remaining 6,666 retain ISO status"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under Section 422(d), the aggregate FMV of stock for which ISOs first become exercisable in any calendar year cannot exceed $100,000 (measured at grant). At $15/share, 6,666 shares ($99,990) qualify as ISOs. The remaining 1,334 shares are automatically treated as NQSOs. The limit is applied in the order options were granted. Wrong (A): The $100,000 annual limit is exceeded ($120,000 total), so not all options can retain ISO status. Wrong (C): Only the excess over $100,000 is recharacterized, not the entire grant. Wrong (D): The ordering rule gives ISO treatment to the first shares in order; the excess at the end becomes NQSOs.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q3: medium / L2 — Section 409A short-term deferral exception
(6, 'Individual Tax Planning: Compensation', $EXPL$Oakmont Financial accrues a $75,000 performance bonus for analyst Derek Soto on December 31, 2026. The company pays the bonus on February 15, 2027. Derek's tax advisor evaluates whether Section 409A applies to this arrangement. What is the correct analysis?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Section 409A applies because the bonus was not paid in the same taxable year it was earned", "The bonus is exempt only if Derek made a deferral election before January 1, 2026", "The bonus is exempt from Section 409A under the short-term deferral exception because payment was made within 2.5 months after year-end", "The bonus triggers a 20% additional tax because it was paid after the close of the service period"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under Treas. Reg. 1.409A-1(b)(4), compensation paid by the 15th day of the third month following the end of the taxable year is exempt from Section 409A as a short-term deferral. February 15 falls within the 2.5-month window after December 31, so no 409A compliance is required. Wrong (A): The short-term deferral exception specifically permits cross-year payment within the 2.5-month window. Wrong (B): No deferral election is needed for amounts paid within the short-term deferral period. Wrong (D): The 20% penalty applies only to 409A violations; timely payment within 2.5 months is not a violation.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q4: medium / L3 — SAR Section 409A compliance requires base price at or above FMV
(6, 'Individual Tax Planning: Compensation', $EXPL$Riverton Corp grants VP of Operations Naomi Wells 5,000 cash-settled stock appreciation rights on April 1, 2026, when the stock FMV is $32 per share. The company sets the SAR base price at $28 per share to make the grant more attractive. A tax advisor reviews the terms. What Section 409A issue does the advisor identify?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Cash-settled SARs are categorically exempt from Section 409A regardless of the base price", "The SARs are subject to Section 409A solely because they are cash-settled rather than stock-settled", "Setting the base price below FMV is permissible if the discount does not exceed 15% of grant-date FMV", "The SARs must comply with all Section 409A requirements because the base price is below FMV at grant"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under Treas. Reg. 1.409A-1(b)(5), SARs are exempt from Section 409A only if the exercise price equals or exceeds FMV on the grant date. The $28 base price is below the $32 FMV, so these SARs constitute deferred compensation subject to 409A deferral election, distribution, and anti-acceleration rules. Wrong (A): SARs are not categorically exempt; they must meet specific conditions including a base price at or above grant-date FMV. Wrong (B): Cash settlement is a factor, but the primary disqualifying issue here is the discounted base price below FMV. Wrong (C): No 15% safe harbor discount exists for SARs under Section 409A; that discount applies to ESPPs under Section 423.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q5: medium / L2 — Section 83 applies to property transfers, not unfunded cash promises
(6, 'Individual Tax Planning: Compensation', $EXPL$Sterling Consulting promises associate director Ryan Kemp a $100,000 cash bonus payable in three years if he remains employed. No trust or escrow is established. Ryan asks his CPA whether he can make a Section 83(b) election to recognize income currently. What is the correct advice?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["A Section 83(b) election is not available because Section 83 applies only to transfers of property, not unfunded cash promises", "A Section 83(b) election is available because the bonus is subject to a substantial risk of forfeiture", "A Section 83(b) election is available but only if the employer consents in writing", "A Section 83(b) election is not available because the bonus amount exceeds the $50,000 statutory threshold"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Section 83 governs the taxation of property transferred in connection with the performance of services. An unfunded promise to pay cash in the future is not a transfer of property and is instead governed by Section 409A or general income principles. The 83(b) election requires a property transfer. Wrong (B): Although the bonus has a forfeiture condition, Section 83(b) requires a transfer of property such as stock, which has not occurred here. Wrong (C): Employer consent is not required for an 83(b) election, and the election is unavailable here regardless because no property was transferred. Wrong (D): There is no $50,000 threshold for Section 83(b) elections; the issue is the absence of a property transfer.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q6: medium / L2 — ESPP disqualifying disposition income calculation
(6, 'Individual Tax Planning: Compensation', $EXPL$Calloway Industries employee Priya Venkatesh purchases 300 shares through a Section 423 ESPP on December 1, 2025, when FMV is $40 per share. The ESPP provides a 15% discount, so she pays $34 per share. The offering period began June 1, 2025. She sells all shares on March 15, 2027, for $52 per share. How is the gain reported?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$5,400 ordinary income and no capital gain", "$1,800 ordinary income and $3,600 long-term capital gain", "$5,400 long-term capital gain with no ordinary income component", "$1,800 ordinary income and $3,600 short-term capital gain"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): This is a disqualifying disposition (sold before 2 years from June 1, 2025 offering date). Under Section 423, ordinary income equals the spread at purchase: $40 FMV minus $34 price = $6 per share times 300 = $1,800. The remaining gain of $12 per share ($52 minus $40) times 300 = $3,600 is long-term capital gain (held over one year from purchase). Wrong (A): $5,400 reflects the offering-date discount formula used for qualifying dispositions, not the disqualifying disposition rule. Wrong (C): The purchase discount must be recognized as ordinary income; the entire gain cannot be capital gain. Wrong (D): The capital gain is long-term because the holding period from December 2025 to March 2027 exceeds one year.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q7: medium / L2 — Section 162(m) once covered always covered post-TCJA
(6, 'Individual Tax Planning: Compensation', $EXPL$Ridgecrest Inc., a publicly traded corporation, pays former CFO Darren Webb $1,500,000 in 2026 as deferred compensation under his employment agreement. Webb served as CFO from 2018 to 2024 and has been retired since January 2025. How much may Ridgecrest deduct?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$1,500,000, because Section 162(m) does not apply to former employees", "$0, because all deferred compensation to retired executives is permanently nondeductible", "$1,000,000, because Webb remains a covered employee under the post-TCJA Section 162(m) rules", "$1,500,000, because the $1,000,000 limit applies only to current-year salary"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under Section 162(m) as amended by TCJA, once an individual becomes a covered employee (CEO, CFO, or top three highest-compensated officers), covered status is permanent for all future taxable years, including after retirement. Ridgecrest may deduct only $1,000,000 of the $1,500,000 payment. Wrong (A): Post-TCJA covered employee status does not terminate at retirement or separation from service. Wrong (B): Deferred compensation is deductible when paid under Section 404(a)(5), subject to the $1,000,000 per-person cap. Wrong (D): The $1,000,000 limit applies to all forms of remuneration paid to a covered employee, not just salary.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q8: hard / L4 — ISO vs NQSO when employer has NOLs and no taxable income
(6, 'Individual Tax Planning: Compensation', $EXPL$NovaStar Robotics, a startup with $12 million in accumulated net operating losses and no projected taxable income for at least five years, plans to grant stock options to its CTO. The stock FMV is $10 per share, and the CTO expects it to reach $60 within four years. The tax advisor must select between ISOs and NQSOs considering both employer and employee tax consequences. What is the most tax-efficient recommendation?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["NQSOs, because the employer deduction at exercise will offset the company's net operating losses and generate a refund", "NQSOs, because the CTO will receive long-term capital gain treatment on the entire spread at exercise", "ISOs, because the employer is required to pass through a deduction that the CTO can claim on a personal return", "ISOs, because the NQSO employer deduction has no current value, and ISOs provide the CTO potential long-term capital gain treatment"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): ISOs generate no ordinary income at exercise under Section 421, and the entire gain qualifies as LTCG if holding periods are met. The NQSO employer deduction under Section 83(h) is worthless to NovaStar because it has no taxable income to offset. ISOs maximize combined after-tax value when the employer cannot use the deduction. Wrong (A): The deduction merely increases the existing NOL; it cannot generate a refund because NovaStar already has excess losses with no income to offset. Wrong (B): NQSOs produce ordinary income at exercise, not capital gain; capital gain treatment applies only to post-exercise appreciation. Wrong (C): No pass-through deduction mechanism exists for ISOs; the employer receives no deduction for qualifying ISO dispositions.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q9: medium / L3 — ISO nontransferability requirement
(6, 'Individual Tax Planning: Compensation', $EXPL$Alderton Corp grants executive Lisa Tran 10,000 ISOs. For estate planning purposes, Tran wishes to transfer 5,000 of the options to an irrevocable family trust during her lifetime. She asks her tax advisor whether this is permissible without affecting the ISO classification. What advice should the advisor provide?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The transfer would disqualify only the 5,000 transferred options, which would be reclassified as NQSOs; the retained 5,000 remain ISOs", "The transfer is permitted because family trusts are not considered separate taxpayers for ISO purposes", "The transfer is permitted if the trust beneficiaries are Tran's lineal descendants", "The transfer would disqualify all 10,000 options because any attempted transfer taints the entire grant"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under Section 422(b)(5), ISOs must be nontransferable during the holder's lifetime other than by will or the laws of descent and distribution. Transferring 5,000 options to a trust violates this requirement for those options, reclassifying them as NQSOs. The 5,000 options Tran retains are unaffected. Wrong (B): Irrevocable trusts are separate legal entities; lifetime transfers to them violate the ISO nontransferability rule under Section 422(b)(5). Wrong (C): No exception exists for transfers to trusts of lineal descendants; the nontransferability rule is absolute during the holder's lifetime. Wrong (D): Only the transferred options lose ISO status; the retained options continue to meet all ISO requirements.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q10: hard / L4 — Section 409A change-in-control ownership threshold
(6, 'Individual Tax Planning: Compensation', $EXPL$Grandview Medical Group maintains a Section 409A NQDC plan that permits lump-sum distribution upon a change in control. A private equity firm acquires 35% of Grandview's outstanding voting stock. Existing shareholders collectively retain 65% of the vote. The plan administrator asks a tax advisor whether this triggers a permissible distribution. What is the advisor's determination?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["A change in control has occurred because any acquisition exceeding 25% of voting stock qualifies under Section 409A", "No change in ownership has occurred because Section 409A requires acquisition of more than 50% of total voting power for that test", "A change in control has occurred because the 30% threshold under the effective control presumption is met", "Distributions are permitted because the PE firm holds a blocking minority position"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under Treas. Reg. 1.409A-3(i)(5)(v), a change in ownership occurs when a person or group acquires more than 50% of the total fair market value or voting power of the corporation. A 35% acquisition does not meet this threshold. The 30% threshold referenced in choice C applies to the separate "change in effective control" test, which requires additional factors beyond mere stock ownership. Wrong (A): There is no 25% threshold under the Section 409A change-in-control regulations. Wrong (C): The 30% presumption under the effective control test requires a pattern of acquiring stock over a 12-month period along with board turnover, not a single acquisition. Wrong (D): Blocking minority status has no relevance to the Section 409A change-in-control definitions; only statutory tests apply.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q11: easy / L2 — ESPP maximum offering period
(6, 'Individual Tax Planning: Compensation', $EXPL$Lakeview Corp is designing a new Section 423 employee stock purchase plan and proposes a 36-month offering period with a standard 15% discount. The company's tax advisor reviews the terms. What compliance issue does the advisor identify?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["No issue exists because there is no statutory maximum offering period for Section 423 ESPPs", "The offering period cannot exceed 12 months under Section 423", "The offering period exceeds the 27-month maximum under Section 423(b)(7) and would disqualify the plan", "The offering period cannot exceed 24 months under IRS safe harbor rules"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Section 423(b)(7) limits the offering period to a maximum of 27 months when the purchase price is based on the lesser of FMV at the beginning or end of the offering period with a standard discount. A 36-month offering period exceeds this limit and would disqualify the plan. Wrong (A): Section 423(b)(7) imposes a specific maximum duration on the offering period. Wrong (B): Twelve months is a commonly used offering period but is not the statutory maximum. Wrong (D): Twenty-four months is frequently used in practice but the statutory ceiling is 27 months.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q12: medium / L3 — Section 4999 excise tax computation base
(6, 'Individual Tax Planning: Compensation', $EXPL$Pinnacle Corp enters a change-in-control agreement with CEO Thomas Yu. Yu's base amount (average W-2 compensation for the prior five taxable years) is $300,000. Upon a qualifying change in ownership, Yu receives total parachute payments of $1,200,000. The tax advisor computes the Section 4999 excise tax. What is the correct excise tax amount?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$240,000, computed as 20% of the total $1,200,000 parachute payments", "$60,000, computed as 20% of the $300,000 amount exceeding three times the base amount", "$120,000, computed as 20% of the amount exceeding two times the base amount", "$180,000, computed as 20% of the excess parachute payment of $900,000"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under Section 4999, the 20% excise tax applies to the excess parachute payment, defined as total parachute payments minus one times the base amount. Excess = $1,200,000 minus $300,000 = $900,000. Tax = 20% times $900,000 = $180,000. The three-times-base test ($900,000) merely determines whether the excise applies at all. Wrong (A): The excise tax applies only to the excess over 1x base amount, not the total payment. Wrong (B): This incorrectly uses the 3x threshold as the tax base; $1,200,000 minus $900,000 = $300,000 times 20% = $60,000 is not the correct computation. Wrong (C): No 2x base amount threshold exists under Section 280G or Section 4999.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q13: easy / L1 — Section 132(m) qualified retirement planning services
(6, 'Individual Tax Planning: Compensation', $EXPL$Under Section 132(m), which employer-provided benefit is excluded from an employee's gross income?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Retirement planning advice provided by the employer to employees and their spouses", "Personal financial planning covering investment portfolio management", "Estate planning and wealth transfer services for senior executives", "Individual income tax return preparation for all employees"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Section 132(m) excludes qualified retirement planning services from gross income when provided by an employer that maintains a qualified retirement plan. The exclusion covers retirement-specific advice for employees and their spouses but does not extend to broader financial, estate, or tax preparation services. Wrong (B): Investment portfolio management is a general financial service, not a qualified retirement planning service under Section 132(m). Wrong (C): Estate planning and wealth transfer services are outside the scope of the Section 132(m) exclusion. Wrong (D): Tax return preparation is not a qualified retirement planning service under Section 132(m).$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q14: medium / L2 — FICA withholding on NQSO vs ISO exercise
(6, 'Individual Tax Planning: Compensation', $EXPL$Ashford Technologies grants Chief Marketing Officer Elena Navarro 5,000 ISOs and 5,000 NQSOs, both with an exercise price of $20. She exercises all 10,000 options when FMV is $55 per share. The payroll department asks the tax advisor which exercises require employer FICA withholding. What is the correct answer?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Both the ISO and NQSO exercises require FICA withholding on the $35 per share bargain element", "Only the NQSO exercise requires FICA withholding; the ISO exercise does not trigger FICA", "Neither exercise requires FICA withholding because FICA applies only when the shares are later sold", "Only the ISO exercise requires FICA withholding due to the AMT preference item at exercise"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under Section 3121(a)(22), the spread on ISO exercise is specifically excluded from FICA wages. NQSO exercises produce ordinary income under Section 83 that is subject to both income tax withholding and FICA. The NQSO bargain element of $175,000 (5,000 times $35) requires FICA withholding. Wrong (A): ISO exercises are explicitly excluded from FICA wages by Section 3121(a)(22). Wrong (C): NQSO income is subject to FICA at exercise when the spread is recognized as ordinary income, not at a later sale. Wrong (D): The AMT preference item from ISOs does not create any FICA obligation; FICA is based on regular tax wages.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q15: medium / L3 — S corp reasonable salary reclassification employment tax exposure
(6, 'Individual Tax Planning: Compensation', $EXPL$Kendra Holmes is the sole shareholder-employee of an S corporation that earns $240,000 in net income. She pays herself a $60,000 salary and takes $180,000 in distributions. A CPA determines that a reasonable salary would be $120,000. If the IRS reclassifies $60,000 of distributions as wages, what is the approximate additional employment tax exposure?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$0, because S corporation distributions are never subject to employment tax reclassification", "$4,590, representing only the additional employee share of FICA", "$9,180, representing the combined employer and employee FICA on $60,000 of reclassified wages", "$18,360, representing 15.3% applied to the full $120,000 reasonable salary"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Reclassifying $60,000 of distributions as compensation triggers both employer FICA (7.65%) and employee FICA (7.65%) on the additional wages: $60,000 times 15.3% = $9,180. Under Section 3121(d), S corporation officer compensation must be reasonable, and the IRS routinely challenges artificially low salaries. Wrong (A): The IRS has authority to reclassify distributions as wages when officer compensation is unreasonably low. Wrong (B): $4,590 accounts for only the employee share (7.65%); the employer share is also owed, doubling the total exposure. Wrong (D): FICA was already paid on the $60,000 original salary; only the $60,000 shortfall is subject to reclassification.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q16: hard / L4 — Section 83(i) qualified stock election for private company employees
(6, 'Individual Tax Planning: Compensation', $EXPL$Vertex Labs, a privately held startup with 50 employees, grants engineer Maya Patel 10,000 shares of restricted stock that vest after three years. The shares are not tradeable on any established market. Vertex has a written equity plan covering at least 80% of all U.S. employees. At vesting, FMV is $45 per share. Maya's tax advisor evaluates a Section 83(i) election. What is the maximum deferral period and the key limitation?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["3 years from vesting, and the deferred amount is subject to a Section 409A 20% penalty upon inclusion", "7 years from vesting, and the income is converted from ordinary income to long-term capital gain", "Indefinite deferral until the stock is sold or the company goes public, with no character conversion", "5 years from vesting or earlier if the stock becomes transferable, and the income remains ordinary when recognized"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Section 83(i) allows eligible employees of qualifying private companies to defer income inclusion for up to 5 years after vesting, or earlier if the stock becomes transferable or publicly traded. The deferred income retains its ordinary character when ultimately recognized. The employer must grant equity to at least 80% of U.S. employees under a written plan. Wrong (A): The deferral period is 5 years, not 3, and Section 83(i) operates independently of Section 409A. Wrong (B): Section 83(i) does not convert ordinary income to capital gain; the deferred amount remains ordinary income at recognition. Wrong (C): The deferral is limited to 5 years or an earlier liquidity or transferability event; it is not indefinite.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q17: hard / L3 — ISO AMT basis vs regular tax basis on qualifying sale
(6, 'Individual Tax Planning: Compensation', $EXPL$Clearpoint Dynamics employee Jared Thornton exercises ISOs for 2,000 shares at $15 per share when FMV is $40. He meets both the two-year grant and one-year exercise holding periods, then sells all shares for $55 per share. His regular tax gain is $80,000 ($55 minus $15 times 2,000). What is his gain for AMT purposes on this qualifying sale?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$30,000, because AMT basis includes the $40 FMV at exercise rather than the $15 exercise price", "$80,000, because AMT and regular tax use the same exercise price basis for ISO shares", "$0, because the AMT preference item recognized at exercise eliminates all future AMT gain", "$50,000, using the midpoint between the exercise price and FMV at exercise as the AMT basis"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): For AMT purposes, the ISO basis includes the bargain element recognized as an AMT preference item at exercise. AMT basis = $40 FMV at exercise. AMT gain = ($55 minus $40) times 2,000 = $30,000. The $50,000 basis difference generates an AMT credit that can offset future regular tax liability exceeding tentative minimum tax. Wrong (B): AMT basis for ISO shares is the FMV at exercise ($40), not the exercise price ($15) used for regular tax. Wrong (C): The AMT preference at exercise increases AMT basis but does not eliminate gain on a subsequent sale above the exercise-date FMV. Wrong (D): No midpoint calculation exists; AMT basis equals the full FMV at the date of exercise.$EXPL$, 'hard', 3),

-- Q18: medium / L3 — Section 404(a)(5) employer NQDC deduction timing
(6, 'Individual Tax Planning: Compensation', $EXPL$Crestview Partners establishes a nonqualified deferred compensation plan for managing director Aiden Cross. Crestview credits $150,000 to Cross's account in 2026 and the account grows to $220,000 by 2031, when Cross receives the full distribution upon separation from service. When does Crestview receive its compensation deduction, and for what amount?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["In 2026 for $150,000, when the amount is credited to Cross's account", "In 2031 for $220,000, when the full amount is includible in Cross's gross income", "In 2031 for $150,000, limited to the original deferral amount excluding investment returns", "No deduction is available because nonqualified deferred compensation is not deductible"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under Section 404(a)(5), the employer's deduction for nonqualified deferred compensation is allowed in the taxable year in which the amount is includible in the employee's gross income. Cross includes $220,000 in 2031, so Crestview deducts $220,000 in that year. The deduction matches the employee's inclusion. Wrong (A): The employer cannot deduct deferred compensation when credited; it must wait until the employee recognizes income. Wrong (C): The deduction includes the full amount the employee includes in income, including investment returns on the deferred amount. Wrong (D): Deferred compensation is deductible under Section 404(a)(5) when the employee includes it in income.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q19: easy / L2 — Section 125 cafeteria plan eligible pretax benefits
(6, 'Individual Tax Planning: Compensation', $EXPL$Bellwood Manufacturing offers a Section 125 cafeteria plan. An employee asks which benefit may be elected on a pretax basis through the plan. Which of the following is an eligible qualified benefit under Section 125?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Gym membership reimbursements", "Contributions to a Roth IRA", "Health insurance premiums and dependent care assistance", "Student loan repayment assistance"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Section 125 permits employees to elect specific qualified benefits on a pretax basis, including employer-provided health coverage under Section 106, dependent care assistance under Section 129, health flexible spending arrangements, and adoption assistance. These elections reduce both income tax and FICA wages. Wrong (A): Gym memberships are not a qualified benefit under Section 125, even if an on-premises facility may be excludable under Section 132. Wrong (B): Roth IRA contributions are made with after-tax dollars by definition and cannot be offered as a pretax election through a cafeteria plan. Wrong (D): Student loan repayment assistance is not among the enumerated qualified benefits under Section 125.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q20: easy / L1 — Section 409A separation from service definition
(6, 'Individual Tax Planning: Compensation', $EXPL$Under Section 409A regulations, what standard determines whether an employee has experienced a separation from service?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The employee must submit a formal written resignation to the employer's board of directors", "The employee must receive a lump-sum severance payment from the employer", "The employee must have reached the plan's stated normal retirement age", "The employee must have a reasonably anticipated permanent reduction in services to 20% or less of prior average levels"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under Treas. Reg. 1.409A-1(h), a separation from service occurs when the employee dies, retires, or otherwise experiences a reasonably anticipated permanent reduction in service level to 20% or less of the average over the preceding 36-month period. This is a facts-and-circumstances determination. Wrong (A): No formal written resignation is required; the regulatory test focuses on actual service levels, not documentation. Wrong (B): A severance payment is a potential consequence of separation, not the determining factor; separation is based on service reduction. Wrong (C): Reaching retirement age does not constitute separation from service if the employee continues performing services at prior levels.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q21: easy / L1 — Section 409A premium interest penalty on plan failures
(6, 'Individual Tax Planning: Compensation', $EXPL$In addition to the 20% additional tax, what interest penalty does Section 409A impose on deferred compensation included in income due to a plan failure?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Interest at the underpayment rate plus one percentage point, accruing from the year the compensation was first deferred", "Interest at the federal short-term rate, accruing from the date of the plan failure", "A flat 5% annual penalty on the total amount of deferred compensation", "No interest penalty; the 20% additional tax is the sole consequence"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Section 409A(a)(1)(B)(ii) imposes an interest charge at the underpayment rate plus 1 percentage point. The interest accrues from the earlier of the taxable year in which the amount was first deferred or, if later, the first year the amount is no longer subject to a substantial risk of forfeiture. This compounds the penalty substantially over long deferral periods. Wrong (B): The applicable rate is the underpayment rate plus 1%, not the federal short-term rate alone, and accrual begins from the deferral year. Wrong (C): No flat 5% penalty exists under Section 409A; the interest is computed on a compounding accrual basis. Wrong (D): Section 409A explicitly imposes both the 20% additional tax and the premium interest charge as dual penalties.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1)
;

